explanation:
The most robust way to handle AWS resources that vary by region and time (like AMIs) is to use Data Sources.

In Terraform, a resource creates something new, but a data source fetches information about something that already exists. By using data "aws_ami", you are telling Terraform: "Go ask AWS what the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI is in the current region right now."

Here is how the code typically looks:

data "aws_ami" "latest_linux" {

most_recent = true

owners      = ["amazon"]

filter {

name   = "name"

values = ["amzn2-ami-hvm-*-x86_64-gp2"]

}

}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {

ami           = data.aws_ami.latest_linux.id

instance_type = "t2.micro"

}

This approach ensures that your infrastructure code remains static, but the actual AMI ID used is always dynamic and up-to-date with the specific region you are deploying into.

Terraform (HCL) supports three distinct styles of commenting, giving developers flexibility in how they document their Infrastructure as Code.

#: The standard single-line comment. Use this for simple explanations or headers.

//: An alternative single-line comment. Valid, but less common than #.

/* ... */: The multi-line block comment. Use this for long descriptions or commenting out entire resource blocks during debugging.

Best Practice: Stick to # for single lines to maintain consistency with the wider Terraform community, and use /* */ when you need to write a paragraph or disable a chunk of code.

